Specific comments on code quality:

The UI interaction logic (mouse state checks and hit-testing against calculated rectangles) is well-executed, however, we should encapsulate some of the repetitive 
rectangle calculations or hit detection into helper methods to reduce code duplication and improve readability.

I actually really like the use of linq for filtering and sorting the items in the shop. It makes the code cleaner and more expressive. The use of a dictionary to store items 
is also a good choice, as it allows for quick lookups.The filtering and sorting logic is clear and easy to follow, which is great for maintainability.

The shop layout system assumes fixed widths and item box padding, which could break if screen size or item count changes unexpectedly. Rather than calculating item rectangles 
inline repeatedly, extract a helper method to generate item rectangles with offsets.

For improved maintainability, consider adding error handling in case resource loading fails (if an icon texture is missing). The item dictionary being directly iterated 
assumes all keys are safe for rendering, but there’s no null check for item icons or content-loaded textures. If a texture is missing, it could cause a runtime exception 
causing the game to crash. 

ShopItem.cs is mostly fine structurally, but it exposes all properties publicly with no validation. If you ever allow editing of prices or amounts post-initialization, 
you’ll want setter validation or a readonly pattern.